## Method and reasoning
Split storage into blocks of size about √n. For a complete block change its offset, and for either partial boundary block change the affected individual entries. A read adds the individual value and its block offset.

## Walk through the sample
Position 3 starts at 3, receives +4 from [2,5], then −2 from [1,3], so its reads are 7 and 5. Position 5 receives only the first update and finishes at 9.

## Complexity
An update touches O(√n) entries or blocks, a read O(1), and space is O(n).

## Reference implementation
~~~
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);
    int n, q;
    cin >> n >> q;
    int size = max(1, int(sqrt(n)));
    vector<long long> a(n), offset((n + size - 1) / size);
    for (auto &x : a)
        cin >> x;
    while (q--) {
        string op;
        int l;
        cin >> op >> l;
        --l;
        if (op == "GET")
            cout << a[l] + offset[l / size] << '\n';
        else {
            int r;
            long long x;
            cin >> r >> x;
            --r;
            while (l <= r && l % size)
                a[l++] += x;
            while (l + size - 1 <= r) {
                offset[l / size] += x;
                l += size;
            }
            while (l <= r)
                a[l++] += x;
        }
    }
}
~~~
